Document.description_en:
Terminal Norcan Inc. operates an oil terminal that supplies numerous service stations in Quebec and Ontario. The facilities are supplied by vessels via two marine pipelines connecting Wharf 74 to the Norcan terminal, located at 6370 Notre-Dame Street East in Montreal, across from Section 66 of the Port of Montreal.

&nbsp;

The work will take place in Section 70 of the Port of Montreal, on lots 1,773,871 and 1,773,873 of the Quebec cadastre, on federal land managed by the Montreal Port Authority.

&nbsp;

The terminal has been in operation since 1935. Over time, the marine pipelines have been relocated several times. Those targeted by the present project are underground, have a diameter of 14 inches, and were installed in 1987. The project involves replacing two underground sections, each 94 meters long, at Section 70 of the Port of Montreal, part of which will be above ground. The work is being carried out as a preventive measure. It should be noted that Terminal Norcan regularly inspects its marine pipelines using an intelligent pig.

&nbsp;

Access to the underground pipelines is constrained by their location alongside rail tracks of the Port of Montreal, as well as beneath the railway siding serving the Catelli company. To access them, it will be necessary to lower the ground level on the north side, which is currently used as a parking area by employees of the company Termont. A 2-meter-high concrete block retaining wall will need to be dismantled and then rebuilt after the work is completed.

&nbsp;

The work will be carried out in the fall of 2026.

Document.description_fr:
La compagnie Terminal Norcan inc. exploite un terminal p&eacute;trolier qui dessert de nombreuses stations-service au Qu&eacute;bec et en Ontario. Les installations sont approvisionn&eacute;es par navires via deux conduites maritimes qui relient le quai 74 au terminal Norcan, situ&eacute; au 6370, rue Notre-Dame Est &agrave; Montr&eacute;al, vis-&agrave;-vis la section 66 du Port de Montr&eacute;al.

&nbsp;

Les travaux auront lieu &agrave; la section 70 du Port de Montr&eacute;al, au droit des lots 1 773 871 et 1 773 873 du cadastre du Qu&eacute;bec, lesquels sont sous la gestion de l&#39;Administration portuaire de Montr&eacute;al.

&nbsp;

Le terminal est en op&eacute;ration depuis 1935. Au fil du temps, les conduites maritimes ont &eacute;t&eacute; relocalis&eacute;es &agrave; plusieurs reprises. Celles vis&eacute;es par le pr&eacute;sent projet sont souterraines, ont un diam&egrave;tre de 14 pouces et ont &eacute;t&eacute; mises en place en 1987. Le projet pr&eacute;voit le remplacement de deux sections souterraines de 94 m&egrave;tres chacune, au droit de la section 70 du Port de Montr&eacute;al, dont une portion sera hors terre. Ces travaux sont r&eacute;alis&eacute;s &agrave; titre pr&eacute;ventif. &Agrave; noter que Terminal Norcan fait inspecter ses conduites maritimes sur une base r&eacute;guli&egrave;re &agrave; l&#39;aide d&#39;un racleur intelligent.

&nbsp;

L&#39;acc&egrave;s aux conduites souterraines est contraint par leur localisation le long de voies ferr&eacute;es du Port de Montr&eacute;al, ainsi que sous la desserte ferroviaire de la compagnie Catelli. Pour y acc&eacute;der, il sera n&eacute;cessaire d&#39;abaisser le niveau du terrain du c&ocirc;t&eacute; nord, actuellement utilis&eacute; comme stationnement par les employ&eacute;s de la compagnie Termont. Un muret de blocs de ciment de 2 m&egrave;tres de haut devra &ecirc;tre d&eacute;mantel&eacute; puis reconstruit apr&egrave;s les travaux.

&nbsp;

Les travaux seront r&eacute;alis&eacute;s &agrave; l&#39;automne 2026.

Document.description:
Terminal Norcan Inc. operates an oil terminal that supplies numerous service stations in Quebec and Ontario. The facilities are supplied by vessels via two marine pipelines connecting Wharf 74 to the Norcan terminal, located at 6370 Notre-Dame Street East in Montreal, across from Section 66 of the Port of Montreal.

&nbsp;

The work will take place in Section 70 of the Port of Montreal, on lots 1,773,871 and 1,773,873 of the Quebec cadastre, on federal land managed by the Montreal Port Authority.

&nbsp;

The terminal has been in operation since 1935. Over time, the marine pipelines have been relocated several times. Those targeted by the present project are underground, have a diameter of 14 inches, and were installed in 1987. The project involves replacing two underground sections, each 94 meters long, at Section 70 of the Port of Montreal, part of which will be above ground. The work is being carried out as a preventive measure. It should be noted that Terminal Norcan regularly inspects its marine pipelines using an intelligent pig.

&nbsp;

Access to the underground pipelines is constrained by their location alongside rail tracks of the Port of Montreal, as well as beneath the railway siding serving the Catelli company. To access them, it will be necessary to lower the ground level on the north side, which is currently used as a parking area by employees of the company Termont. A 2-meter-high concrete block retaining wall will need to be dismantled and then rebuilt after the work is completed.

&nbsp;

The work will be carried out in the fall of 2026.

Document.description_en:
As part of a City-wide drainage improvements project, the City of Ottawa (City) is looking to improve roadway drainage along Linden Terrace. This location, adjacent to 298 Queen Elizabeth Drive, currently discharges roadway stormwater runoff to Patterson Creek via three (3) 100mm diameter storm sewers that travel from Linden Terrace to the creek through Patterson Creek Park (NCC property). As part of this project, the City of Ottawa would like to abandon two (2) existing storm sewers, while replacing them with one (1) new storm sewer discharging to Patterson Creek. A new alignment through the park has been proposed to minimize impacts to existing vegetation and mature trees.

&nbsp;

Portions of the project would take place on National Capital Commission (NCC) lands and are &nbsp;subject to a federal land use and design approval (FLUDA) under the National Capital Act and an environmental effects determination under Section 82 of the Impact Assessment Act. Consultation with the following federal departments will also be required:


	Fisheries and Oceans Canada (DFO): as Patterson Creek is deemed fish habitat, a self-assessment will be undertaken to determine if a Letter of Review from DFO will be required.
	Parks Canada Agency (PCA): The Rideau Canal, and possibly Patterson Creek, are considered lands administered by PCA, and as such, a PCA&nbsp; In-Water or Shoreline Works Approval may be required.
	&nbsp;Transport Canada for navigable waterway self-assessment.


&nbsp;

The work along Linden Terrace would be completed within the existing roadway with a right-of-way owned by the City of Ottawa. The new 250mm diameter storm sewer is proposed to be constructed through Patterson Creek Park, which is an urban park that features mature trees, vegetation, gardens, walking paths, a bistro, and a creek (Patterson Creek) that flows into the adjacent Rideau Canal.

&nbsp;

The construction is currently scheduled to begin in fall 2027 following the seasonal lowering of the water level within the Rideau Canal and Patterson Creek. It is anticipated that the majority of construction activities will take one to two (1-2) months to complete, with landscaping reinstatement to be completed in spring 2028 over the course of one (1) week. The construction area will be accessed with light duty machines and equipment, and all work areas will be fenced accordingly. The use of hydrovac excavation equipment will be used to complete required excavations within close proximity to trees and other sensitive features. Construction work will abide by all municipal by-laws, including noise.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The proposed alignment of the new storm sewer will avoid the need to remove trees. Grass or other vegetated areas impacted by construction will be replaced with topsoil and sod following construction. The abandonment of the existing storm sewers within the park will be completed by filling them with cementitious grout, which will save trees and shrubs that have been planted within and adjacent to their alignments. Temporary detouring of traffic along the pathway north of Patterson Creek, that runs through the park, will be required to construct the storm sewer to the new outfall location. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

Document.description_fr:
Dans le cadre d&#39;un projet d&#39;am&eacute;lioration du r&eacute;seau de drainage &agrave; l&#39;&eacute;chelle de son territoire, la Ville d&#39;Ottawa compte am&eacute;liorer le drainage routier le long de la terrasse Linden. &Agrave; cet endroit, soit &agrave; proximit&eacute; du 298, promenade Queen Elizabeth, les eaux pluviales provenant de la chauss&eacute;e sont actuellement &eacute;vacu&eacute;es vers le ruisseau Patterson par trois &eacute;gouts pluviaux de 100&nbsp;mm de diam&egrave;tre qui relient la terrasse Linden au ruisseau en traversant le parc du ruisseau Patterson (propri&eacute;t&eacute; de la Commission de la capitale nationale). La Ville d&#39;Ottawa profitera de l&#39;occasion pour abandonner deux &eacute;gouts pluviaux et les remplacer par un nouvel &eacute;gout pluvial qui se d&eacute;versera dans le ruisseau Patterson. Un nouveau trac&eacute; &agrave; travers le parc a &eacute;t&eacute; propos&eacute; pour limiter les r&eacute;percussions sur la v&eacute;g&eacute;tation et les arbres matures.

&nbsp;

Certaines parties du projet, qui se d&eacute;rouleraient sur des terrains de la Commission de la capitale nationale, doivent faire l&#39;objet d&#39;une approbation f&eacute;d&eacute;rale de l&#39;utilisation du sol et du design en vertu de la Loi sur la capitale nationale ainsi que d&#39;une &eacute;valuation des effets environnementaux en vertu de l&#39;article&nbsp;82 de la Loi sur l&#39;&eacute;valuation des impacts. Les minist&egrave;res f&eacute;d&eacute;raux suivants devront &eacute;galement &ecirc;tre consult&eacute;s.


	P&ecirc;ches et Oc&eacute;ans Canada&nbsp;: le ruisseau Patterson &eacute;tant consid&eacute;r&eacute; comme un habitat du poisson, une auto-&eacute;valuation devra &ecirc;tre r&eacute;alis&eacute;e afin de d&eacute;terminer s&#39;il est n&eacute;cessaire d&#39;obtenir une lettre d&#39;examen de la part de P&ecirc;ches et Oc&eacute;ans Canada.
	Agence Parcs Canada&nbsp;: le canal Rideau et le ruisseau Patterson sont consid&eacute;r&eacute;s comme des terres administr&eacute;es par l&#39;Agence; &agrave; ce titre, une autorisation de l&#39;Agence pour des travaux dans l&#39;eau ou sur les berges pourrait &ecirc;tre requise.
	&nbsp;Transports Canada pour l&#39;auto-&eacute;valuation relative aux voies navigables.


&nbsp;

Les travaux le long de la terrasse Linden seraient r&eacute;alis&eacute;s dans les limites de l&#39;actuelle chauss&eacute;e, sur une emprise appartenant &agrave; la Ville d&#39;Ottawa. L&#39;&eacute;gout pluvial de 250&nbsp;mm de diam&egrave;tre propos&eacute; traverserait le parc du ruisseau Patterson, un parc urbain comprenant des arbres matures, de la v&eacute;g&eacute;tation, des jardins, des sentiers p&eacute;destres, un bistrot et un ruisseau (le ruisseau Patterson) qui se jette dans le canal Rideau adjacent.

&nbsp;

Le d&eacute;but des travaux est pr&eacute;vu pour l&#39;automne&nbsp;2027, apr&egrave;s l&#39;abaissement saisonnier du niveau d&#39;eau dans le canal Rideau et le ruisseau Patterson. On pr&eacute;voit que la majeure partie des travaux sera termin&eacute;e en un &agrave; deux mois. La remise en &eacute;tat des espaces verts devrait quant &agrave; elle &ecirc;tre effectu&eacute;e au printemps&nbsp;2028 en l&#39;espace d&#39;une semaine. Le chantier accueillera de la machinerie et des &eacute;quipements l&eacute;gers, et toutes les zones o&ugrave; des travaux seront effectu&eacute;s seront cl&ocirc;tur&eacute;es en cons&eacute;quence. De l&#39;&eacute;quipement d&#39;hydroexcavation sera utilis&eacute; &agrave; proximit&eacute; imm&eacute;diate des arbres et d&#39;autres zones sensibles. Les travaux respecteront l&#39;ensemble des r&egrave;glements municipaux, notamment en mati&egrave;re de bruit.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Le trac&eacute; propos&eacute; pour le nouvel &eacute;gout pluvial permettra d&#39;&eacute;viter l&#39;abattage d&#39;arbres. Les zones couvertes de gazon ou d&#39;autre v&eacute;g&eacute;tation touch&eacute;es par les travaux seront remises en &eacute;tat avec de la terre v&eacute;g&eacute;tale et du gazon en plaque apr&egrave;s la fin des travaux. Les &eacute;gouts pluviaux qui seront mis hors service dans le parc seront remplis de coulis, ce qui permettra de pr&eacute;server les arbres et arbustes plant&eacute;s &agrave; l&#39;int&eacute;rieur et &agrave; proximit&eacute; de leur trac&eacute;. Pendant la construction de l&#39;&eacute;gout pluvial jusqu&#39;au nouvel emplacement de l&#39;exutoire, le sentier situ&eacute; au nord du ruisseau Patterson, qui traverse le parc, devra &ecirc;tre d&eacute;tourn&eacute; temporairement.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

