Document.description_en:
Mniku (Chapel Island), Potlotek First Nation, Cape Breton, Nova Scotia

&nbsp;

Mniku is approximately 200 m offshore at the north end of Sitmuk Awti (Chapel Island Road), off of Highway 4, in Potlotek. The Project site is located along the eastern shoreline of the southern portion of Mniku, and is accessible by way of watercraft launched at a small wharf located at the north end of Sitmuk Awti.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Mniku is a National Historic Site of cultural, spiritual, and archaeological importance to the Mi&#39;kmaq. Portions of the Mniku shoreline are lowlying and vulnerable to coastal erosion and flooding. Shoreline retreat has resulted in the exposure of sacred burial grounds and culturally significant artifacts, highlighting the sensitivity of the site and the need for erosion mitigation measures that protect both physical landforms and irreplaceable cultural heritage. Coastal hazards at Mniku have intensified over time due to increasing storm activity, elevated water levels, and climate change impacts, with notable erosion damage occurring during major storm events such as Hurricane Fiona in September 2022. These processes continue to pose a risk to culturally sensitive shoreline areas, particularly along shores actively used for community and ceremonial activities.

&nbsp;

The proposed design is considered a hybrid nature-based infrastructure solution which pairs a rock revetment along the shoreline on Mniku with planted vegetation features on the crest and landward side of the revetment. The entire revetment structure will be constructed on the landward side of the delineated Eelgrass habitats along the shoreline of Mniku to avoid directly impacting these habitats, and will be constructed above the higher-high water large tide elevation for this site. There are no planned site preparation activities along the shoreline due to the presence of graves and archaeological resources; the materials used to construct the revetment will be placed directly overtop of the existing shoreline materials, and there are no requirements to relocate existing structures or vegetation along the existing shoreline. The total footprint area of the structure is 650 m2.

&nbsp;

Eskasoni First Nation, Cape Breton, Nova Scotia

&nbsp;

The physical activity at this location is to be carried out on federal lands in relation to a physical work and requires a&nbsp;Canadian Navigable Waters Act (CNWA) approval. Therefore, Transport Canada is a federal authority for the Eskasoni location only.

&nbsp;

Portions of the Eskasoni shoreline along Shore Road are relatively low-lying and are vulnerable to coastal erosion and flooding. Erosion of the shoreline has led to shoreline retreat and over time this process has the potential to pose a risk to the portion of the shore in the project footprint area which is used recreationally (e.g., swimming, fishing) by the community.

&nbsp;

The proposed design is considered a hybrid nature-based infrastructure solution that pairs three low-crested rock sills with landward sediment infill and planted vegetation features on the landward side of the revetment. The sills dissipate wave energy generated within the Bras d&#39;Or Lake before it reaches the shoreline which reduces the erosive forces acting on the shoreline and backshore areas. Small gaps between each sill allow for water circulation and sediment exchange while maintaining effective wave attenuation. This proposed configuration of sills and sediment infill is well suited to the mild to moderate, fetch-limited wave conditions present at the site.

&nbsp;

The proposed Project design avoids overlap or interaction with the delineated wetland or Eelgrass habitats during or following construction. Overall, the project footprint area for the three sills and coarse sand infill is 980 m2.

Document.description_fr:
Mniku (Chapel Island), Premi&egrave;re Nation de Potlotek, Cap-Breton, Nouvelle-&Eacute;cosse

&nbsp;

Mniku est situ&eacute;e &agrave; environ 200 m au large, &agrave; l&#39;extr&eacute;mit&eacute; nord de Sitmuk Awti (Chemin Chapel Island), &agrave; partir de la route 4, &agrave; Potlotek. Le site du projet se trouve le long du rivage est de la portion sud de Mniku et est accessible par embarcation &agrave; partir d&#39;un petit quai situ&eacute; &agrave; l&#39;extr&eacute;mit&eacute; nord de Sitmuk Awti.

&nbsp;

Mniku est un lieu historique national d&#39;importance culturelle, spirituelle et arch&eacute;ologique pour les Mi&#39;kmaq. Certaines portions du littoral de Mniku sont de faible &eacute;l&eacute;vation et vuln&eacute;rables &agrave; l&#39;&eacute;rosion c&ocirc;ti&egrave;re et aux inondations. Le retrait du littoral c&ocirc;tier a entra&icirc;n&eacute; l&#39;exposition de lieux de s&eacute;pulture sacr&eacute;s et d&#39;art&eacute;facts culturellement significatifs, ce qui met en &eacute;vidence la sensibilit&eacute; du site et la n&eacute;cessit&eacute; de mesures d&#39;att&eacute;nuation de l&#39;&eacute;rosion qui prot&egrave;gent &agrave; la fois les formes physiques du terrain et un patrimoine culturel irrempla&ccedil;able. Les risques c&ocirc;tiers &agrave; Mniku se sont intensifi&eacute;s au fil du temps en raison de l&#39;augmentation de l&#39;activit&eacute; des temp&ecirc;tes, de l&#39;&eacute;l&eacute;vation du niveau de l&#39;eau et des impacts des changements climatiques, avec des dommages d&#39;&eacute;rosion importants observ&eacute;s lors d&#39;&eacute;v&eacute;nements majeurs tels que l&#39;ouragan Fiona en septembre 2022. Ces &eacute;v&egrave;nements continuent de pr&eacute;senter un risque pour les zones c&ocirc;ti&egrave;res culturellement sensibles, particuli&egrave;rement le long des rivages utilis&eacute;s activement pour des activit&eacute;s communautaires et c&eacute;r&eacute;monielles.

&nbsp;

La conception propos&eacute;e est consid&eacute;r&eacute;e comme une solution hybride d&#39;infrastructure naturelle, combinant un enrochement le long du rivage de Mniku avec des &eacute;l&eacute;ments de v&eacute;g&eacute;tation plant&eacute;e au sommet et du c&ocirc;t&eacute; terrestre de l&#39;enrochement. L&#39;ensemble de la structure sera construit du c&ocirc;t&eacute; terrestre des habitats de zost&egrave;re d&eacute;limit&eacute;s le long du littoral de Mniku afin d&#39;&eacute;viter tout impact direct sur ces habitats, et sera r&eacute;alis&eacute; au-dessus du niveau des plus hautes grandes mar&eacute;es pour ce site. Aucune activit&eacute; de pr&eacute;paration du site n&#39;est pr&eacute;vue le long du rivage en raison de la pr&eacute;sence de s&eacute;pultures et de ressources arch&eacute;ologiques ; les mat&eacute;riaux utilis&eacute;s pour construire l&#39;enrochement seront d&eacute;pos&eacute;s directement sur les mat&eacute;riaux existants du littoral, et aucun d&eacute;placement de structures ou de v&eacute;g&eacute;tation existantes n&#39;est requis. La superficie totale de l&#39;empreinte de la structure est de 650 m&sup2;.

&nbsp;

Premi&egrave;re Nation d&#39;Eskasoni, Cap-Breton, Nouvelle-&Eacute;cosse

&nbsp;

L&#39;activit&eacute; concr&egrave;te &agrave; cet emplacement sera r&eacute;alis&eacute;e sur un territoire domanial et est li&eacute;e &agrave; un ouvrage n&eacute;cessitant une approbation en vertu de la Loi sur les eaux navigables canadiennes (LENC). Par cons&eacute;quent, Transports Canada est une autorit&eacute; f&eacute;d&eacute;rale uniquement pour l&#39;emplacement d&#39;Eskasoni.

&nbsp;

Certaines portions du littoral d&#39;Eskasoni, le long de Shore Road, sont relativement basses et vuln&eacute;rables &agrave; l&#39;&eacute;rosion c&ocirc;ti&egrave;re et aux inondations. L&#39;&eacute;rosion du rivage a entra&icirc;n&eacute; un retrait du littoral c&ocirc;tier et, avec le temps, ce ph&eacute;nom&egrave;ne pourrait poser un risque pour la portion du littoral situ&eacute;e dans l&#39;empreinte du projet, laquelle est utilis&eacute;e &agrave; des fins r&eacute;cr&eacute;atives (p. ex. baignade, p&ecirc;che) par la communaut&eacute;.

&nbsp;

La conception propos&eacute;e est consid&eacute;r&eacute;e comme une solution hybride d&#39;infrastructure naturelle, combinant trois seuils rocheux &agrave; cr&ecirc;te basse avec un remblai s&eacute;dimentaire du c&ocirc;t&eacute; terrestre et des &eacute;l&eacute;ments de v&eacute;g&eacute;tation plant&eacute;e sur le c&ocirc;t&eacute; terrestre de l&#39;enrochement. Les seuils rocheux dissipent l&#39;&eacute;nergie des vagues g&eacute;n&eacute;r&eacute;es dans le lac Bras d&#39;Or avant qu&#39;elles n&#39;atteignent le rivage, ce qui r&eacute;duit les forces &eacute;rosives agissant sur le littoral et l&#39;arri&egrave;re-plage. De petits espaces entre chaque seuil permettent la circulation de l&#39;eau et l&#39;&eacute;change de s&eacute;diments tout en maintenant une att&eacute;nuation efficace des vagues. Cette configuration propos&eacute;e de seuils rocheux et de remblai s&eacute;dimentaire est bien adapt&eacute;e aux conditions de vagues faibles &agrave; mod&eacute;r&eacute;es, pr&eacute;sentes sur le site.

&nbsp;

La conception du projet propos&eacute;e &eacute;vite tout chevauchement ou interaction avec les habitats de milieux humides ou de zost&egrave;res d&eacute;limit&eacute;s, pendant et apr&egrave;s les travaux. La superficie totale de l&#39;empreinte de la structure est de 980 m&sup2;.

